{
  "schema": "vegnourish.palette/1",
  "note": "What color a dish is. Not decoration assigned at random — a tomato dish should be red because it is full of tomatoes, and the same recipe has to look the same every time you open it. So the color comes out of the ingredient list, weighted by how much of each thing is in the pot and by how strongly that thing actually colors what it is in.",
  "why": "A wall of identical cards is a wall nobody scans. Giving each dish the color of its own dominant ingredient means the collection becomes recognizable by sight — you learn that the deep red one is the shakshuka before you read the title — and it costs nothing, because the app already knows exactly what is in every dish.",
  "howItWorks": [
    "Each color group names a hue and the ingredients that carry it, with a strength: how hard that ingredient pushes its color onto a dish, per gram. Tomato paste is ferocious, a can of tomatoes is strong, a broth is nearly nothing.",
    "A recipe's score for a group is the sum of grams times strength across its members. The strongest group is the card's color; the runner-up tints the far corner of the gradient, so two red dishes still differ from each other.",
    "Nothing scores? The cuisine decides, and failing that the course does. Every dish gets a color and none of them are random."
  ],
